WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Command injection in Bash is best understood as a boundary failure.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:11.240
A value that should remain data starts to influence command structure, shell syntax, or execution flow.

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:19.420
The value might be a file name, mode, service name, environment label, identifier, or option value.

4
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:27.680
When a script treats that value as part of a command line instead of passing it as data, behavior becomes harder to predict and harder to review.

5
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:35.860
When a script treats a runtime value as part of a command line instead of passing it as data, behavior becomes harder to predict and harder to review.

6
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.380
This matters because Bash interpretation is powerful.

7
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:49.440
Bash expands variables, splits words, expands patterns, handles redirection, builds pipelines, and starts commands.

8
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.260
Those features are useful when the script author controls them intentionally.

9
00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.040
They are risky when runtime values are allowed to shape the shell syntax that Bash interprets.

10
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.160
Bash scripts often sit in operational workflows with real authority.

11
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:14.180
They run in CI/CD jobs, backup tasks, deployment scripts, maintenance automation, scheduled cleanup routines, and administrative helper tools.

12
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:22.920
A small parsing or construction mistake can affect files, services, releases, logs, artifacts, or infrastructure state.

13
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:29.600
The defensive goal is not to make Bash unusable or to treat every script as suspicious.

14
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.260
The goal is to make command execution explicit.

15
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.480
A safe script should run known commands, with intended arguments, in a controlled context.

16
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:42.540
If an input is not supported, the script should reject it before it reaches high-impact command logic.

17
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:51.380
A good mental model is to separate three things: the command chosen by the script, the shell syntax written by the author, and the data provided at runtime.

18
00:01:51.480 --> 00:02:00.360
Prevention is strongest when the first two are stable and reviewable, while runtime data is validated and passed as arguments rather than interpreted as shell language.

19
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.400
Throughout this course, we will stay at a defensive engineering level.

20
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:19.620
The emphasis is on recognizing trust boundaries, avoiding risky construction patterns, preserving argument boundaries, validating inputs, controlling execution context, and using review practices that catch mistakes before scripts become trusted automation.
